    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 84257,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84257.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "of people. And our party is getting engaged in the process of thinking about replacing the Republican president in 2020, whether it's Donald Trump or Mike Pence or anybody else. And we need to go out and win that election. You know, the question of high crimes and misdemeanors of the president or the 25th Amendment on fitness of the president to serve are analytically distinct from who do we want to put into office in 2020. But isn't it a matter of energy? Well, and absolutely, and everything in politics is a trade-off. And so where's our energy going to go? Where's our attention going to go? What are we going to focus on? But look, leaving impeachment aside, we've got a serious constitutional oversight duty to the American people, which we intend to execute and to vindicate. You know, those are things that we need to be checking out. Obviously the Mueller report hasn't come back yet. Those prosecutions are still underway and we have a lot more information to find out about and we have a lot of abuses of power that we need to correct. So we're committed to doing that and You know, for me, impeachment should be neither a fetish nor a taboo. Nobody should be obsessed with it. It's not the be on the end all in any way.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
